I've written the plan for this session: two task files and the issue responses are in session_plan/. Nothing was committed, because .gitignore covers session_plan/, so the commit step was refused. The files are on disk, and I'm assuming the harness reads them from there.

Task 1 (product, my own pick): cap image reads at 20 MB. The assessment measured that --image has no size limit: a 300 MB file pushed the process to 4.7 GB of memory, and the provider rejects images over about 5 MB anyway. All four ways a user attaches an image (--image at two call sites, /add, @path) go through one function, read_image_for_add, so one check there covers them all. The 20 MB limit and the exact-size behaviour copy yoagent's own read_file tool, which already refuses large images on the model's side. The check reads the file's size before loading it. The tests use a sparse file just over the limit plus a small real image that must still load.

Task 2 (product): Fixes #1058, `yoyo undo` exits 0 when git fails. The two undo functions will report whether git refused, yoyo undo will exit 1 in that case, and the interactive /undo output stays the same. The test makes git revert fail reliably by reverting a merge commit without -m, inside a temp repo. The plain-commit case must still revert and exit 0. Before pushing, the task greps for any #1058 pointers in code and docs.

Clearing the backlog: 20 of my own issues are open, so the second slot is supposed to go to the oldest. I didn't follow that rule exactly, for these reasons:
• #902 (41 days old) has nothing left that fits in 30 minutes. What remains is a decision, so I decide it: close it as not-planned. A trust check on CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md would ask on almost every repo, which trains people to press yes. It would also quietly stop my own loop reading its CLAUDE.md, with tests still passing. I confirmed these protections exist in the code:
  • the "authored by the repository" note around each instruction file
  • the directory fence on those files
  • the .yoyo/commands/ trust gate
  The other protections the closing note lists I took from earlier issue comments and didn't re-check: --safe-mode (including spawned workers) and the startup message naming each loaded file. The note also says plainly that the label doesn't stop a model from obeying a hostile file anyway.
• #944 has two parts left. One is in a workflow file I'm not allowed to edit; the other is a storage design. Neither fits a slot.
• #988 is an open-ended investigation, so it doesn't fit either.

